5.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"General Fund reserve at minimum with only \u00a355k buffer; Corporate Projects reserve being drawn to cover current-year deficit","lead":"A council running reserves at minimum with no buffer cannot absorb unexpected costs or income shortfalls; further draws on restricted reserves signal financial stress.","iso":"2026-02-10","paper_title":"Budget Report 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40135/40135.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-02-10","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Budget Report 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40135/40135.pdf","headline":"Balanced 2026/27 budget significantly supported by non-recurring or volatile government grant uplifts that do not extend into years 2 and 3","detail":"The \u00a33.083m favourable movement that eliminated the original \u00a31.928m deficit includes \u00a31.410m from FFR uplift (based on recognition of temporary accommodation costs in funding formula) and \u00a3743k from EPR grant outperformance (council received \u00a31.080m against MTFS assumption of \u00a30.337m). Neither of these movements are reflected in Years 2\u20133 forecasts, and the MTFS still shows gaps of \u00a3206k (Year 2) and \u00a3373k (Year 3).","category":"income","matters":"The balanced budget is materially dependent on grant income that the council has not assumed will continue; Years 2\u20133 structural gaps suggest the council faces renewed pressure once these improve once-off benefits unwind.","figures":"FFR uplift Year 1 \u00a31.410m (\u00a30m Years 2\u20133); EPR grant uplift \u00a30.743m Year 1 only; three-year gap reduced from \u00a35.063m to \u00a30.579m (Years 2\u20133 still \u00a3579k cumulatively)","quote":"During the summer of 2025, changes to the provisional government funding formula, particularly the recognition of actual expenditure on temporary accommodation, indicated that the Council might benefit rather than lose funding...the Council ultimately received \u00a31.080m, a positive movement of \u00a30.743m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-02-10","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Budget Report 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40135/40135.pdf","headline":"Budget balance dependent on \u00a31.35m annual dividend from two commercial properties held by wholly-owned subsidiary outside the borough","detail":"The council explicitly relies on dividend from EEPIC (Epsom & Ewell Property Investment Company) to balance the budget. The two commercial properties are held outside the borough and were acquired in 2017 before new statutory guidance restricted such acquisitions. The council holds \u00a35.7m Property Income Equalisation Reserve specifically to mitigate loss of this income.","category":"income","matters":"Property income is volatile and concentrated in a single subsidiary; tenant default, property value decline, or market conditions could force immediate reserve drawdown at a council already running at minimum buffers.","figures":"\u00a31.35m dividend expected 2026/27; \u00a35.7m Property Income Equalisation Reserve held against loss of income","quote":"The Council's 2025/26 budget includes \u00a31.35m expected dividend income from Epsom and Ewell Property Investment Company, generated from its two commercial properties. This is on track to be fully received this financial year and will continue into 2026/27. The council relies on dividend from its commercial properties to balance the budget. In the event of loss of dividend, the council holds prudent level of Property Income Equalisation Reserve, \u00a35.7m, to counter the loss.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-02-10","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Budget Report 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40135/40135.pdf","headline":"General Fund reserve at minimum with only \u00a355k buffer; Corporate Projects reserve being drawn to cover current-year deficit","detail":"The General Fund working balance is \u00a31.555m against a \u00a31.5m minimum, leaving only \u00a355k flexibility. The 2025/26 projected \u00a3520k deficit is being funded from the Corporate Projects reserve, depleting it from \u00a32.495m to \u00a31.975m. Uncommitted reserves available for general use drop from \u00a36.593m to \u00a33.530m after commitments.","category":"reserves","matters":"A council running reserves at minimum with no buffer cannot absorb unexpected costs or income shortfalls; further draws on restricted reserves signal financial stress.","figures":"General Fund \u00a31.555m vs \u00a31.5m minimum (\u00a355k buffer); Corporate Projects reserve reduced by \u00a3520k from \u00a32.495m to \u00a31.975m; total uncommitted reserves (General Fund + Corporate Projects) \u00a33.530m","quote":"The General Fund reserve currently stands at \u00a31.555m, only \u00a355,000 above the \u00a31.5m minimum balance approved by Council in July 2025. Therefore, it is suggested that this deficit is met from the Corporate Projects reserve, decreasing the uncommitted balance from \u00a32,495,000 to \u00a31,975,000.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-02-10","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Budget Report 2026/27","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40135/40135.pdf","headline":"Temporary accommodation demand and costs creating ongoing pressure despite budget increase","detail":"The council projected a \u00a3520k deficit in 2025/26 mainly from higher demand for housing and homelessness services. To address this, it has increased nightly paid accommodation budget by \u00a3750k over the MTFS period (\u00a3250k annually), yet forecast pressures of \u00a3250k per year continue into Years 2 and 3, suggesting costs are outpacing the increase.","category":"demand","matters":"Rising demand for high-cost services creates structural budget pressures that may exceed reserves if growth continues beyond forecast or if government resets FFR benefits.","figures":"\u00a3520k projected 2025/26 deficit; \u00a3750k budget increase over MTFS; \u00a3250k annual pressure Years 2\u20133; net budget increase in Community & Wellbeing from \u00a35.798m to \u00a37.161m (1.363m increase)","quote":"The projected deficit is mainly due to the higher demand for housing/homelessness services...Community & Wellbeing Committee agreed to recommend service estimates for 2026/27 to Full Council...which include a net increase of budget for nightly paid accommodation costs of \u00a3750,000 to address the rising cost in this area.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-02-10","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Budget Report 2026/27 Appendix 11","paper_url":"https://democracy.epsom-ewell.gov.uk/documents/s40146/40146.pdf","headline":"Health & Safety Executive investigation into December 2021 waste collection vehicle incident could result in settlement payment with provision set aside","detail":"The council has made a provision for the outcome of an HSE investigation into a December 2021 incident involving a waste collection vehicle. This outstanding matter could result in a settlement payment that would impact the waste collection budget (c.\u00a31.4m income).","category":"other","matters":"An unresolved regulatory matter with financial exposure that could materialise during the 2026/27 budget year.","figures":"c.\u00a31.4m waste collection income at risk; provision set aside (amount not disclosed)","quote":"The Health & Safety Executive (HSE) investigation into December 2021's incident involving a waste collection vehicle could result in a settlement payment. Provision set-aside for outcome of HSE investigation.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-16"},"name":"Epsom & Ewell","findings_total":60,"news_drafts_total":3}}